#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063958 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "4bbaf6b0-1057-5485-a3d8-2126e74fecdc" :question/math-academy-id "e-3717" :question/problem "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x) = x^2$ on the interval $[0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$  is a polynomial, so it is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$ \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c \\in (0,2)$ such that \n\n$$\nf'(c)   = \\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(0)}{2-(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf'(c) = 2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =2 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063959 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ed796d54-235b-5ec0-b73f-5396d1ef99b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3718" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ below is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ \\, .$ *\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/5672cc68e55078623f34f01650d3e82bd3cee62e6ca97fabcd2287f2a865501a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b),$ the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c \\in (a,b)$ such that\n$f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.$*\n\nIn our case, we have $a = -1$ and $b=4,$ so the above statement becomes \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-1)}{4-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2}{4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that* $f'(c) =\\dfrac {4}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063960 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "d47e7afd-bed0-5f2b-8635-ddd1a5303837" :question/math-academy-id "e-3719" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[−2,2]?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/e680c2e80874a4c2e8b4d31fcc7a6ba42a3c55fb7c6f559e42f3cd785b22e3ed.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{I}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the sharp corners at $x=\\pm 2$ are not in $(-2,2).$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{II}$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2.$ So it is not continuous on $[-2,2],$ and therefore it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{III}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that although the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2,$ it is right-continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{IV}$ is continuous on $[−2,2]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=0.$ So, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(−2,2).$  Therefore, it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nIn conclusion, only diagrams $\\textrm{I}$ and $\\textrm{III}$ show functions that satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064036 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "92bba9e0-62db-5c96-8cbc-3cfbe8863a94" :question/math-academy-id "e-6738" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)= 3^{x} - 1$\n\n- $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$\n\n- $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 3^{x} - 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x = 0,$ hence, it is not continuous at \n$x = 0,$ and so it is not continuous on $[0,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df0c4859-5dea-5999-8d14-38364fcd450d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314139} {:answer/id #uuid "a062fd1a-a751-5522-9439-7c2d18fd2251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314140} {:answer/id #uuid "24c7d6cd-76f1-5136-b989-3e280d92b241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314141} {:answer/id #uuid "75e29608-d101-5e74-a0f9-aeb93dacd868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314142} {:answer/id #uuid "19ceeb92-e248-5e16-81d4-f63546d4c29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(c)=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24c7d6cd-76f1-5136-b989-3e280d92b241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314141} :answer-field/id #uuid "121e061c-8f59-5d38-a696-c7283ddd23ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70a9e682-5225-5573-9ac6-75c97731ce3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-125237" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/74850221ee168c6911ee5b6c319d2128f67149f81790528940822e253905fc0f.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-5,3]$ and differentiable on  $(-5,3).$  What is the missing part of the sentence in the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-5$ and $b=3.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-5)}{3-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-2}{3+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb037793-c00e-51a0-b7e9-19ff2a8b5b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/b246b2835d856e4ec43bd9a96275ddf5531b7d653d46e5c58ae9553b6b59956e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314160} {:answer/id #uuid "28f4edb0-b2e0-5f2d-b31b-e20351847375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/45db5f9f600f29e91fb40335f80fb28bb12f87fc4618c623354b91408f6bb09c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314161} {:answer/id #uuid "aee0cde4-4274-5c11-a1a6-35951fd3a6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/c4c45b3d88dd9821c1c5650e898d806b8887838c609548296040feae8915e40b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314162} {:answer/id #uuid "babd29ad-332f-5bf1-a097-5410fc085503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/09017ce160ebb2995e0c7c4efe81fd5444ba8cedd91dc270c69757b8b0eb1ae4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314163} {:answer/id #uuid "bf563876-8bda-570b-8deb-7aae372ff247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/e50696609559e7996dda87b3566639e0f1772b0538b9b41e8311cb49b5c41a5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb037793-c00e-51a0-b7e9-19ff2a8b5b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/b246b2835d856e4ec43bd9a96275ddf5531b7d653d46e5c58ae9553b6b59956e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314160} :answer-field/id #uuid "43e165b1-ef88-519e-80a6-6a1ee85a6ea2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89cdcb08-8a88-5ba3-bb82-4bb65262eb4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-125317" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $g(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, $h(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1.$ Hence, $p(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1.$ Hence, $s(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba9b3820-2c88-588c-8149-74f4311067b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/d5927b5cc43ee1b5923b4d2aa4b4740e2bbc1c73b09b00525cb74767badc4808.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335678} {:answer/id #uuid "5193fae4-9ca9-5844-a705-8cd04c15abae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/787772f6d68af4df373cbfdd6b7a3cf3d313db7e03ecce36437f01068b738408.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335679} {:answer/id #uuid "233c301c-2915-5218-992c-ef218e3bd6ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/f5d7744d62f94b96b8bd1600ca7f5fc752d1f83c8c8928e740261e808cb9e07b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335680} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c00683-baab-5743-97b8-4035232dabdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/8d37dc99a006ba89bc6a874d251a065fc714b260072740206f9cc3692b08c604.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335681} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9acf66-d993-5a0e-87cd-0752623c602c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/4c9f5d5245a1a4aa33a0d474ff328a30a1c8820055551e3613bf7c9489c2a908.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9acf66-d993-5a0e-87cd-0752623c602c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/4c9f5d5245a1a4aa33a0d474ff328a30a1c8820055551e3613bf7c9489c2a908.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335682} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3519f6d-17c0-5e34-805e-2f1cf9252804" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48cd65ad-908c-58d2-aca2-a54d10434503" :question/math-academy-id "q-127011" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $m(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $n(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $n(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $l(x)$  has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $l(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)$  has removable discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c81cbbd4-4e7c-5e03-93f6-fab148bd5f7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314167} {:answer/id #uuid "be200f29-1998-5d37-bf05-22e22786efd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314168} {:answer/id #uuid "99a27541-8b8e-5648-a51c-72fd6996b406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314169} {:answer/id #uuid "58bb6da6-8fe0-5437-a563-3216c42738e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314170} {:answer/id #uuid "028e6e8b-8793-5ec9-91d1-c87713cb4e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be200f29-1998-5d37-bf05-22e22786efd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314168} :answer-field/id #uuid "468bce35-a6af-512c-a602-d116db5280cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d78e600b-4326-5596-a935-0576240419b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-127013" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)=2|x|$\n- $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- $h(x)=|x+1|$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1],$ but it has a corner in $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=1-x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $h(x)=|x+1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x+1$ over the given interval. Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only functions II and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5416af2-c5a6-5183-8a75-14862a9fb852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5416af2-c5a6-5183-8a75-14862a9fb852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314195} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a11827e-ce9d-5354-a735-fdc0d1a904b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba9aff4f-f2e6-5d6a-bbda-2b487e4d8919" :question/math-academy-id "q-345063" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x)=5\\sqrt{x}-2$ on the interval $[4,9].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[4,9]$ and differentiable on $(4,9),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (4,9)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=4$ and $b=9$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(9)-f(4)}{9-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5\\sqrt{9}-2)-(5\\sqrt{4}-2)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-8}{5} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\sqrt{x}-2)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{c}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{c}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{c} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ c & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b969cfd-7cc1-55a7-9880-7b61b434f5b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/b629cd9686bb3f9b671a9412552ccd4161cb832d8e8426ee83a8955b81cc5831.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314198} {:answer/id #uuid "4a02ae06-70c4-57f4-a3e2-1626b85c36e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/6cd605b5533bf0211bf75d3cc1d461fe41ee382e8799bd2337c4a53627eb0ca9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314199} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4727d0-5f06-5481-a558-a7dfce526bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/0680d1da0bbcc0d40668bef248d55441acd77c1304545d6710256eb023023b05.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314200} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdbb879-2681-5110-b7e0-15eab3076414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/ec5215bf37cce6a5b6a6f6cddcc6366e757ae464bdbde83c7276ca2db85d64cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314201} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf4d318-7ea5-5e1e-8222-640f35d2ff3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/6283b99ea5ee48cf8dbbf56297a03d29e7de3a09ed3fdeac9376b3a875d045d6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bdbb879-2681-5110-b7e0-15eab3076414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/ec5215bf37cce6a5b6a6f6cddcc6366e757ae464bdbde83c7276ca2db85d64cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314201} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f453603-c815-5323-a538-420797e8c5c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8670d161-54b2-5d76-9f17-77b18f090a9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-48396" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,4]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function  $g(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$   Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(1,4).$  Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$ and is not defined for $x\\in (-\\infty ,1].$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[-1,4].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Hence, $h(x)$  is not continuous on $[-1,4].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $f(x)$  is continuous on $[-1,4]$ but has a cusp at $x=2.$  Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c246d93-1a7e-5ac0-bc7c-075112e3ff7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314212} {:answer/id #uuid "ff342493-7ac1-5c81-9e90-11e24667c2dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314213} {:answer/id #uuid "350d3a49-76f7-5443-8488-eac6e90e4e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314214} {:answer/id #uuid "99185650-3aca-5788-80ed-0299e529036e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314215} {:answer/id #uuid "db8e3319-4087-53ea-ab8e-545a388b1106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db8e3319-4087-53ea-ab8e-545a388b1106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314216} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7271472-91ee-5dab-9fa6-a3d4527d95f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad37b0b8-9076-54d3-a5c8-dee0a591a349" :question/math-academy-id "q-48497" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4]?$\n\n- $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- $f(x)={x}^{4}$\n- $h(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[0,4]$ but has a corner at $x=1.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,4).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{4}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ is not defined on $(-\\infty ,1).$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,4].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function II satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97c18829-6760-54bb-b25f-d10c4378b1cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(c) = \\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062952} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c8907c-e186-5f42-8744-97afb6b8f647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062953} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1f2090-b915-5e6c-b1e5-56c49951daec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(c) = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062954} {:answer/id #uuid "462aeff4-a2c8-50d9-a6f6-4392570ccc4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(c) = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062955} {:answer/id #uuid "658bae93-2830-5aeb-a093-977c4122ea56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(c) = \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "658bae93-2830-5aeb-a093-977c4122ea56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(c) = \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062956} :answer-field/id #uuid "848fdf88-1fad-5e42-a7f9-34f9bc0e79ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062951}] :question/id #uuid "bb1393df-f078-5d0d-b6e1-3a860ee39d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48554" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/288/Source/Images/q-48554.png)\n\nThe function $y = f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[- 2, 4]$ and differentiable on $(-2, 4)$. Complete the sentence below.\n\n> *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c ∈ (-2, 4)$ such that $\\mid _______$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc868210-90c8-5aef-8a96-299b3c7236db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314226} {:answer/id #uuid "dd764a30-26f8-5243-9774-99f981dcac04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314227} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e66a85-4cec-555e-926c-267fb5074e37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314228} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7a81f8-6c26-5bac-a646-1a6beefe08f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(c)=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314229} {:answer/id #uuid "9c20212e-f576-5a79-9ba2-8ed2dbe1b52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd764a30-26f8-5243-9774-99f981dcac04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314227} :answer-field/id #uuid "52164d79-ea96-5cd6-a8b0-6bd7485d4f2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37d9c80f-2cd5-546e-9463-84b02d5e64b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-73349" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/6393810d90b8d340dbde041c43cb7e146cb59fedfcfd4a678827d291ea502b00.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-5,4]$ and differentiable on  $(-5,4).$  What is the missing part of the sentence in the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,4)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following: \n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-5$ and $b=4.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-5)}{4-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-2}{4+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,4)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{9}.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "807eff2f-33f9-5678-87ed-4ec523447a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314233} {:answer/id #uuid "16c1ff25-221b-595c-b770-a6c70a3c76a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314234} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8443f2-f2bb-5db0-93f6-66a50a9920df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314235} {:answer/id #uuid "7aad2e0d-d3c8-552f-a49e-c35b2a0b0b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314236} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2d3ddb-93d5-5d4d-9b86-b078b3600fa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(c)=-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aad2e0d-d3c8-552f-a49e-c35b2a0b0b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314236} :answer-field/id #uuid "0422f357-1fa3-5fa9-9b29-4197de680874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75e8bebf-29a4-5c2b-8823-7c997148cfbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-73354" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/029a51dccba9f1ec927c820fb8adb72e926b88a881f4646090ac651ef29da271.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-5,3]$ and differentiable on $(-5,3).$ Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-5$ and $b=3.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-5)}{3-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6}{3+5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{3}{8}.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca80d048-3584-53f5-8bb1-1f66a7c3c2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/db518cdc174cb245f2479f44a185266f274d9c6f2b5ef90f3d752aadf761726d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314247} {:answer/id #uuid "f4dff307-835e-50bb-a926-eb8ec8f9f146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/535745a110dcf5c361965127a8d25455687415c547f8ee7406d0add33121589b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314248} {:answer/id #uuid "d35f131b-276b-5124-9132-da7921b90833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/ef3499bbe65662a44c92b3c5ce5023bfa74b8cbd2f37dc182bdc62dbcffba1f3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314249} {:answer/id #uuid "2e684ecb-b0c3-5233-aa04-ae52638e236f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/49db2f4f9b88672a0d3a8229f80850b6187f2cebc59a8a334f869d3ffa7a56a6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314250} {:answer/id #uuid "5e09b422-07ff-5ae6-b667-d158ea9e29a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/266cc36afab26e5af24044f66b8689b3abc550409d40336375e896064b1badf3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca80d048-3584-53f5-8bb1-1f66a7c3c2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/db518cdc174cb245f2479f44a185266f274d9c6f2b5ef90f3d752aadf761726d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314247} :answer-field/id #uuid "b85ffea3-7ffa-59c3-baf6-91fdff82d3bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58937f0c-40a0-5a69-a5bf-d0139202fa4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-77888" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $h(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,3].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ but has a corner point at $x=2.$ Hence, $p(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Hence, $r(x)$  is not continuous on $[-1,3].$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$ Hence, $t(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,3).$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}]}
